# #06.93 Phase 2: `::Foo` resolves to the GLOBAL `Foo`, not to a
# module-inner `Foo` of the same name.
#
# Setup: a top-level `class Foo` AND a `module M { class Foo end }`.
# Three helper functions exercise each lookup variant. All three
# must resolve clean at typeck:
#
#   1. take_global_via_root_anchor — `::Foo` is unambiguously the
#      top-level Foo.
#   2. take_module_qualified       — `M.Foo` is unambiguously the
#      inner Foo (relies on Phase 1's qualified-key registration).
#   3. take_unqualified            — bare `Foo`. Phase 1 keeps the
#      un-qualified entry at top-level, so this also resolves.
#      Phase 4 will change this behaviour to inner-first inside
#      module bodies; the top-level reference here remains valid.
#
# The fact that all three resolve in Phase 2 is the pin. The
# discriminating bit — that `::Foo` and `M.Foo` resolve to DIFFERENT
# DefIds — only becomes user-observable once Phase 3's method
# dispatch lands; for Phase 2 we pin the type-position parser +
# resolver plumbing.

class Foo
end

module M
  class Foo
  end
end

def take_global_via_root_anchor(_x: ::Foo) -> Int
  0
end

def take_module_qualified(_x: M.Foo) -> Int
  0
end

def take_unqualified(_x: Foo) -> Int
  0
end
